WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.789
Stewart: Well when you came along with

00:00:01.798 --> 00:00:04.708
the Mars Trilogy, there were a couple things

00:00:04.708 --> 00:00:06.658
that were unique at the time. One was

00:00:06.658 --> 00:00:09.629
Mars was not hip then. It's insanely hip now.

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:15.239
And you were writing a pretty

00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:19.768
leftist economic model onto basically

00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:21.629
how the geo-engineering or the terraforming

00:00:21.629 --> 00:00:25.049
of Mars would play out. Is that an accurate--

00:00:25.049 --> 00:00:28.559
KSR: Yeah yeah, that's right. Stewart: And uh in your

00:00:28.559 --> 00:00:30.149
mind were you doing that because that's how you

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:32.219
could realistically imagine Mars

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:34.409
actually being terraformed or this was

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:37.679
at last a platform to play out

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:40.409
some of your leftist fantasies?

00:00:40.409 --> 00:00:45.598
KSR: Well maybe more the latter. When I was

00:00:45.598 --> 00:00:47.458
writing the Mars books it was the Viking

00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:49.888
data. The Viking data came in in '76. And

00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:51.208
they gave us a Mars that was a

00:00:51.208 --> 00:00:52.978
million times more detailed than what we

00:00:52.978 --> 00:00:55.258
had before that. Mariner and Viking. It took

00:00:55.258 --> 00:00:56.909
a while to assimilate, for science fiction

00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:58.769
writers. And to say, well what have we got here?

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:02.389
And then it was Sagan and his crowd that said:

00:01:02.389 --> 00:01:03.709
if you were interested in terraforming and

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:05.759
wanted a planet to do it, then Mars is as good as

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:07.799
it gets. You will never find a place that

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:10.139
is more close to being terraformable.

00:01:10.139 --> 00:01:12.179
And they began to model it. So i was

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:14.158
the lucky recipient of that when i began to

00:01:14.158 --> 00:01:16.768
write novels. And I saw that Mars

00:01:16.769 --> 00:01:19.669
would be a great place to backpack and it resembled the High Sierra

00:01:19.789 --> 00:01:22.309
which it didn't. But in Green Mars and Blue Mars

00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:25.109
it does. So...
Stewart: It's Nevada that it resembles, right?

00:01:25.109 --> 00:01:26.939
KSR: Yeah it goes like from the Ruby range in Nevada

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:29.459
to the Sierra to the Coastal range over the

00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:33.009
trilogy. Um, it was something I wanted to do.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:35.269
And then the leftist ideas was just my

00:01:35.279 --> 00:01:39.448
my hippie California, Gary Snyder

00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:43.319
New Age, Buddhist, you know, you know the

00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:46.769
drill here I am!
Stewart: Gary was a red diaper baby.

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:49.369
He grew up with it. Did you?
KSR: No no

00:01:49.378 --> 00:01:51.509
(haha). I grew up in Orange County to a...

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:55.588
a blue diaper baby. Whatever, it was not a

00:01:55.588 --> 00:01:57.298
political household. It was a technical

00:01:57.298 --> 00:02:00.389
and a pseudo apolitical household.

00:02:00.389 --> 00:02:03.449
Stewart: Pseudo apolitical household?

00:02:03.449 --> 00:02:05.609
KSR: Well, I think that the American

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:08.159
technical class would say (it happens too

00:02:08.159 --> 00:02:10.769
often) that well, I'm just into technical

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:12.899
stuff. I transcend politics. Or politics

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.939
is the grubby stuff and Science is the

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:16.859
clean stuff, and they try to make an

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:18.629
artificial distinction between the two.

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:21.029
Stewart: So are you basically turning against that?

00:02:21.029 --> 00:02:23.429
Did somebody get to you with a more

00:02:23.429 --> 00:02:26.519
interesting and persuasive frame?
KSR: It was a

00:02:26.519 --> 00:02:28.559
combination of Snyder, Fredric Jameson,

00:02:28.559 --> 00:02:31.289
Ursula Le Guin, and the

00:02:31.289 --> 00:02:32.969
science fiction community itself. I was

00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:34.589
very lucky in my teachers: all three of

00:02:34.589 --> 00:02:36.209
those were teachers of mine at different

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:39.719
times in life. Jameson was my thesis advisor

00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:41.699
and he told me to write about Philip K

00:02:41.699 --> 00:02:43.469
Dick, the greatest living American writer,

00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:47.009
at which my jaw dropped. I had only read

00:02:47.009 --> 00:02:49.139
Galactic Pot-Healer, and I was looking at

00:02:49.139 --> 00:02:50.999
this professor thinking you've gotta be

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:54.929
kidding me. But i went ahead and i read

00:02:54.929 --> 00:02:57.509
Phil Dick on Fred Jameson's behalf. And then

00:02:57.509 --> 00:03:00.569
Gary was teaching poetry at Davis when i

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:03.239
was there. And so I took classes from him

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:05.219
and Ursula came down to UCSD and taught

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:06.959
science fiction writing at UCSD

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:07.979
so, I'm thinking:

00:03:07.979 --> 00:03:26.649
just do what they tell you. And have fun.

